    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d321">

                <pb facs="0648" n="552" sameAs="frus:frus1874/pg552"/>

                <head>No. 319.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Davis to Sir Edward
                        Thornton.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1874-04-04"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 4,
                        1874</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With the view to enable this Department to
                    answer an inquiry made by the Secretary of the Treasury upon the subject, I
                    shall be much obliged if you will inform me whether the laws of Canada deny to
                    American vessels the right of transportation of goods and passengers <pb
                        facs="0649" n="553" xml:id="pg553"/> between Canadian ports to the extent to
                    which it is granted to Canadian vessels plying between ports in this country,
                    where such transportation is not regulated by the provisions of articles 26, 27,
                    or 30, of the treaty of Washington.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>J. C. B. DAVIS,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Acting
                        Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
